--TEST--
A capture declared at a user class implementing `Parses` stands exactly where one declared at `Core\Uuid` stands, and both arrive as the class rather than as the segment's text — `rule:security/route-capture-is-laundered-by-its-type` and `rule:routing/a-capture-narrows-to-a-closed-set`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The roster a capture narrows to ends in a *predicate* rather than in one more
// name: `Core\Uuid` is admitted because it is a class built from text, and so is
// any other class that says so by implementing `Parses`. This case asks the same
// question of the engine's class and of a user's -- what does the program get
// handed -- and asserts they agree in kind, which is the thing a roster listing
// `Core\Uuid` by name could not have said.
//
// `Parses` needs no `use`: it is one of the interfaces the compiler declares for
// every program, beside `Comparable` and `Stringable`.
class Slug implements Parses
{
    public tainted string $text = "";

    public function constructor(tainted string $text)
    {
        $this->text = $text;
    }

    // The one member the contract requires. It accepts anything here, because
    // what this case asks is which *type* the capture arrives at and never what
    // the class does with the text.
    public static function parse(tainted string $s): static
    {
        return new static($s);
    }
}

class Posts
{
    #[Core\Route(path: "/posts/{slug}", method: Core\Http\Method::Get, name: "Posts::show")]
    #[Core\Access(allow: Core\Audience::Public)]
    public function show(Slug $slug): string
    {
        return "one post";
    }

    #[Core\Route(path: "/posts/by-id/{id}", method: Core\Http\Method::Get, name: "Posts::byId")]
    #[Core\Access(allow: Core\Audience::Public)]
    public function byId(Core\Uuid $id): string
    {
        return "one post";
    }
}

// The user's class. `Core\Router\Match::param` answers the union every capture
// converts to, and `Parses` is its last arm, so the value narrows to the class
// the route declared before anything reads it -- not to the text the peer wrote.
?Core\Router\Match $mine = Core\Router::match(Core\Http\Method::Get, "/posts/hello-world");
if ($mine != null) {
    var $capture = $mine->param("slug");
    if ($capture != null) {
        Slug $slug = $capture as Slug;
        echo "a user class arrives as ", $slug->text, "\n";
    }
}

// The engine's own, at the same site and through the same member. The conversion
// happens in a different place -- the matcher performs this one itself, at the
// door -- and the program cannot tell, which is the agreement being asserted.
//
// It is read by rendering rather than by `as`, because `as` names a *declared*
// class to test the value against and `Core\Uuid` is not one
// (`rule:types/conversion`). That the two arms are read two ways is the language's
// own asymmetry and not the router's: what arrives is the class either way.
?Core\Router\Match $engines = Core\Router::match(
    Core\Http\Method::Get,
    "/posts/by-id/123e4567-e89b-12d3-a456-426614174000",
);
if ($engines != null) {
    echo "the engine's class arrives as ", $engines->param("id"), "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
a user class arrives as hello-world
the engine's class arrives as 123e4567-e89b-12d3-a456-426614174000
